 E(CH2-) = 11.68K2 + 8.94J(J + 1)</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">For R-branches: Jj = Ji + 1, DK = 0:</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">DER = EJj(CH2) - EJi(CH2-)</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = 7.94(Ji + 1)(Ji + 1 + 1) - 8.94Ji(Ji + 1)</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = (Ji + 1){7.94(Ji + 1 + 1) - 8.94Ji}</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = (Ji + 1){(7.94- 8.94)Ji + 2(7.94)}</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = (Ji + 1){-Ji + 15.88}</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">For P-branches: Jj = Ji - 1, DK = 0:</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">DEP = EJj(CH2) - EJi(CH2-)</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = 7.94(Ji - 1)(Ji - 1 + 1) - 8.94Ji(Ji + 1)</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = Ji{7.94(Ji - 1) - 8.94(Ji + 1)}</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = Ji{(7.94- 8.94)Ji - 7.94 - 8.94}</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            = Ji{-Ji - 16.88}</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">This indicates that the R branch lines occur at energies which grow closer and closer together as J increases (since the 15.88 - Ji term will cancel).  The P branch lines occur at energies which lie more and more negative (i.e. to the left of the origin).  So, you can predict that if molecule I is CH2- and molecule II is CH2 then the R-branch has a band head and the P-branch does not.  This is observed, therefore our assumption was correct: molecule I is CH2- and molecule II is CH2.</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            f. The band head occurs when = 0.</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">                        = [(Ji + 1){-Ji + 15.88}] = 0</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">                                      = = 0</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">                                      = -2Ji + 14.88 = 0</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">                                    \ Ji = 7.44,  so J = 7 or 8.</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">At J = 7.44:</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            DER = (J + 1){-J + 15.88}</p> <p class="lt-chem-11570">            DER = (7.44 + 1){-7.44 + 15.88} = (8.44)(8